      "math_academy_id": "q-112498",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x-9,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -4 & -12 & -3 & -9 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & 9 \\\\  & -4 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-4{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(4{x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $4{x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(4)(3)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-4(0)^{3}-12(0)^{2}-3(0)-9=-9\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/1d05449fcf2c39749736b58099a32f59ff85566d5ea56d0e4ec61e88da8ce7aa.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
